  "lane": "LIDAR-03 Native-resolution Dual-Product (1 m + 3 m) LiDAR Scarp Channels (Scarplet diffusion inversion + slope-aware templates + Steger sub-pixel lines + Frangi multiscale Hessian vesselness + TIGER 2024 road & canal mask + spatial CV)",
  "hypothesis": "Active faults missing from the USGS/INGENIOUS catalogue \u2014 particularly across low-relief basin floors and piedmont fans \u2014 preserve subtle (0.3-3 m) diffusion-aged scarplets with paired crest-convexity/toe-concavity dipoles, cross-scale 1 m/3 m strike concordance, and sub-pixel Steger/Frangi ridge-valley continuity. Combining locally relief-normalised 1 m (706 tiles) and 3 m (697 tiles + 2 native 3 m pilot DEM tiles) scarp channels with the cached geophysical/radiometric stack, masking TIGER 2024 roads and geometric canals, and calibrating spatial-CV probabilities across relief terciles recovers off-catalogue and withheld faults across all relief zones without confining sensitivity to range fronts.",
  "mechanism": "1) Set (i): Scarplet diffusion inversion (a_hat = 1.5203 S^2/C, kt_hat) + paired curvature dipole 2*sqrt(lapneg*lappos)/(lapneg+lappos) + slope-aware antislope/cross-slope templates (Sare & Hilley 2018; AGU 2015 T31A-2838) fused with 3 m line-persistent scarp height H_3m and template SNR H_3m/(sigma_ctx+0.15) (Bucknam & Anderson 1979; Hilley et al. 2010). 2) Set (ii): Multiscale Frangi Hessian vesselness (MICCAI 1998) and Steger sub-pixel normal second-derivative line extraction (IEEE TPAMI 1998) at 2-20 m scales on slope-detrended surfaces, aggregated to 100 m as scarp_pixel_fraction, snr_weighted_amplitude, orientation_coherence, and dominant_strike. 3) Cultural veto: US Census TIGER/Line 2024 road corridor (100 m) + 4-way geometric canal/field-edge mask. 4) Spatial CV: 4-fold block holdout HistGradientBoostingClassifier with relief-balanced sample weights and within-stratum quantile calibration.",
  "named_non_fault_mimic": "Graded road/railroad prisms, irrigation canal levees, agricultural field edges, fluvial terrace risers, pluvial Lake Lahontan/Dixie shorelines, and single-sided alluvial fan toe aprons.",
